---
Part VIII, Chapter 11: The contents of a book
Package: packages/loom-lang/src/weave/
Language: TypeScript
---

# The contents of a book

A book's contents are its parts and their chapters, in the order a reader takes them. Nobody writes them down. A list kept by hand drifts from the book it describes: a part with no loom behind it, a chapter listed in the wrong place, a number that skips. A chapter already declares where it belongs, though. Its frontmatter names its part and its position, as `Part VIII, Chapter 11: The contents of a book` does, so Loom reads that placement back out of the corpus whenever something needs the contents. The weave is the main reader: it turns the contents into the woven book's navigation.

A corpus may hold several books, each a project of its own, as the chapter "Books" describes. A loom names its book in a `Book:` line, and a loom that names none belongs to the corpus's default book. So the contents of a corpus are a list of books, and each book has contents of its own.

Not every chapter has a number. A part declared with a bare `Part: Work in progress` and chapters declared `Chapter: <title>` is numberless: a group the book carries but does not sequence, ordered by whoever reads it rather than by position. A numberless chapter may also carry a `Status` and a `Priority`, the metadata a roadmap reads. So the same pass that orders the numbered book also gathers the numberless work beside it, and a presentation renders one as a contents list and the other as a board.

## The shape of the contents

A `TableOfContents` is the corpus's own structure: its `books`, the default book first and the named books after it by name. A `TocBook` carries its `name`, empty for the default book, and the `path` of the book loom that opens it, empty when no loom does. It then carries its three zones. Its body is its `parts` in order, followed by its `orphans`, the chapters that name no part. Its `backMatter` lists its discussions.

A `TocPart` carries its `number`, empty when the part is numberless, its `name`, and its `chapters` in order. A `TocChapter` carries its own `number` (again empty when numberless), its `title`, the optional `status` and `priority` a board reads, the optional `language` its frontmatter names, which a menu can mark the chapter by, and the `path` of the loom it came from, so a presentation resolves it to a page. Everything in it is read from frontmatter, or from a loom's first heading when its frontmatter names no title.

A `TocBackMatter` carries a book's discussions in order. A `TocDiscussion` carries its `place`, its own `number` (empty when it has none), its `title`, and the `path` of its loom. The place names the discussion's part: `Part I` when the part has a number, the part's name when it has none, and nothing when the discussion names no part.

=>

export const TocChapterSchema = Schema.Struct({
  number: Schema.String,
  title: Schema.String,
  status: Schema.optional(Schema.String),
  priority: Schema.optional(Schema.String),
  language: Schema.optional(Schema.String),
  path: Schema.String,
})
export type TocChapter = typeof TocChapterSchema.Type

export const TocPartSchema = Schema.Struct({
  number: Schema.String,
  name: Schema.String,
  chapters: Schema.Array(TocChapterSchema),
})
export type TocPart = typeof TocPartSchema.Type

export const TocDiscussionSchema = Schema.Struct({
  place: Schema.String,
  number: Schema.String,
  title: Schema.String,
  path: Schema.String,
})
export type TocDiscussion = typeof TocDiscussionSchema.Type

export const TocBackMatterSchema = Schema.Struct({
  discussions: Schema.Array(TocDiscussionSchema),
})
export type TocBackMatter = typeof TocBackMatterSchema.Type

export const TocBookSchema = Schema.Struct({
  name: Schema.String,
  path: Schema.String,
  parts: Schema.Array(TocPartSchema),
  orphans: Schema.Array(TocChapterSchema),
  backMatter: TocBackMatterSchema,
})
export type TocBook = typeof TocBookSchema.Type

export const TableOfContentsSchema = Schema.Struct({
  books: Schema.Array(TocBookSchema),
})
export type TableOfContents = typeof TableOfContentsSchema.Type

## The builder and its spine

`buildContents` reads the contents out of a corpus, and the weave calls it directly. The weave hands it the `root` it starts from, the corpus loom, which opens the corpus and stands in no book's contents. `buildContents` is a pure function, since reading the frontmatter waits on nothing.

It gathers every part, chapter, and discussion the corpus declares, each with the book its loom names, and `contentsOf` folds one book's share into a `TocBook`. A book's parts come out numbered-first, so the book reads Part I to Part XI and then its numberless parts, however the corpus happened to load. Each part holds the chapters that name it, in order. The orphan chapters follow the parts, ordered as a part's chapters are, and the discussions gather into the back matter, ordered by their parts.

`bookNamesIn` lists the named books, every book that some loom names, by name. The default book leads the list when it holds anything at all, which `holdsAnything` tells, so a corpus written wholly in named books lists those alone.

=>

export const buildContents = (corpus: LoomCorpusAst, root?: Location): TableOfContents => {
  const modules = Array.filter(
    Array.fromIterable(corpus.modules.values()),
    (module) => module.path !== root,
  )
  const declared: Declared = {
    modules,
    parts: dedupeParts(Array.getSomes(Array.map(modules, partDeclOf))),
    chapters: Array.getSomes(Array.map(modules, chapterDeclOf)),
    discussions: Array.getSomes(Array.map(modules, discussionDeclOf)),
  }
  const defaultBook = contentsOf(declared, '')
  return TableOfContentsSchema.make({
    books: [
      ...(holdsAnything(defaultBook) ? [defaultBook] : []),
      ...Array.map(bookNamesIn(modules), (name) => contentsOf(declared, name)),
    ],
  })
}

type Declared = {
  readonly modules: ReadonlyArray<LoomModule>
  readonly parts: ReadonlyArray<PartDecl>
  readonly chapters: ReadonlyArray<Chapter>
  readonly discussions: ReadonlyArray<Discussion>
}

const contentsOf = (declared: Declared, book: string): TocBook =>
  TocBookSchema.make({
    name: book,
    path: Option.getOrElse(
      Option.map(
        Array.findFirst(
          declared.modules,
          (module) => isBookLoom(module) && bookNameOf(module) === book,
        ),
        (module) => module.path,
      ),
      () => '',
    ),
    parts: pipe(
      Array.filter(declared.parts, (decl) => decl.book === book),
      Array.sort(byPart),
      Array.map((decl) =>
        TocPartSchema.make({
          number: decl.number,
          name: decl.name,
          chapters: chaptersUnder(declared.chapters, decl),
        }),
      ),
    ),
    orphans: pipe(
      Array.filter(
        declared.chapters,
        (chapter) => chapter.book === book && chapter.partId === undefined,
      ),
      Array.sort(byNumber),
      Array.map(toTocChapter),
    ),
    backMatter: TocBackMatterSchema.make({
      discussions: pipe(
        Array.filter(declared.discussions, (discussion) => discussion.book === book),
        Array.sort(byDiscussion),
        Array.map(toTocDiscussion),
      ),
    }),
  })

const bookNamesIn = (modules: ReadonlyArray<LoomModule>): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
  pipe(
    Array.map(modules, bookNameOf),
    Array.filter((name) => name !== ''),
    Array.dedupe,
    Array.sort(Order.String),
  )

const holdsAnything = (book: TocBook): boolean =>
  book.parts.length > 0 || book.orphans.length > 0 || book.backMatter.discussions.length > 0

## A chapter's place in the corpus

Every declaration carries its book, and `bookNameOf` reads it: the name a loom's `Book:` line gives, or the empty name of the default book. A part is declared by any loom that names it: a numbered part in an opener's `Part R: name` line, a numberless part in a bare `Part: name`. `partDeclOf` reads that declaration: the part's book, its number, empty when there is none, and its name.

A chapter is any loom with a title that is neither a discussion nor a book loom, which opens its book rather than standing in it. `chapterDeclOf` reads it: the book and the part it belongs to, its own number, its title, its status and priority, and its path. A loom whose frontmatter names no title takes the title of its first heading, as `firstHeadingTitle` reads it, so a loom with no frontmatter still has a place among the orphans. A chapter names its part by number when the part is numbered and by name when it is numberless, so `partId` is one or the other, and absent when the loom declares no part, which makes the chapter an orphan.

=>

type PartDecl = { readonly book: string; readonly number: string; readonly name: string }

type Chapter = {
  readonly book: string
  readonly partId: string | undefined
  readonly number: string
  readonly title: string
  readonly status: string | undefined
  readonly priority: string | undefined
  readonly language: string | undefined
  readonly path: string
}

const bookNameOf = (module: LoomModule): string => Option.getOrElse(bookOf(module), () => '')

const partDeclOf = (module: LoomModule): Option.Option<PartDecl> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter),
    Option.flatMap((frontmatter) =>
      Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(frontmatter.partName), (partName) => ({
        book: bookNameOf(module),
        number: frontmatter.part?.value ?? '',
        name: partName.value,
      })),
    ),
  )

const firstHeadingTitle = (module: LoomModule): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    Array.findFirst(module.doc.sections, (section) => section.heading.title !== undefined),
    Option.flatMap((section) => Option.fromNullishOr(section.heading.title)),
    Option.map((title) => title.source),
  )

const chapterDeclOf = (module: LoomModule): Option.Option<Chapter> => {
  const frontmatter = module.doc.frontmatter
  return frontmatter?.discussion !== undefined || isBookLoom(module)
    ? Option.none()
    : Option.map(
        Option.orElse(
          Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(frontmatter?.title), (title) => title.value),
          () => firstHeadingTitle(module),
        ),
        (title) => ({
          book: bookNameOf(module),
          partId: frontmatter?.part?.value ?? frontmatter?.partName?.value,
          number: frontmatter?.chapter?.value ?? '',
          title,
          status: frontmatter?.status?.value,
          priority: frontmatter?.priority?.value,
          language: frontmatter?.language?.value,
          path: module.path,
        }),
      )
}

const toTocChapter = (chapter: Chapter): TocChapter =>
  TocChapterSchema.make({
    number: chapter.number,
    title: chapter.title,
    status: chapter.status,
    priority: chapter.priority,
    language: chapter.language,
    path: chapter.path,
  })

## Ordering parts and chapters

A part's identity within its book is its number when it has one and its name when it does not, so `identityOf` reads whichever the part carries and a chapter finds its part by the same key. `samePart` adds the book to the identity, since two books may each hold a Part I. `dedupeParts` keeps one declaration per part, since every loom in a numberless part repeats the part's name.

Numbered parts sort by their Roman numeral, numberless parts fall after them by name, and `byPart` orders both at once. `romanValue` reads a Roman numeral as an integer: each letter is worth its unit, less that unit when a larger one follows it, so `IX` is nine and `XII` is twelve. `byNumber` orders a part's chapters the same way: numbered ones by their Arabic number, then numberless ones by title. `chaptersUnder` gathers the chapters of one part and sorts them.

=>

const romanUnits: Record<string, number> = {
  I: 1,
  V: 5,
  X: 10,
  L: 50,
  C: 100,
  D: 500,
  M: 1000,
}

const romanValue = (roman: string): number => {
  const units = Array.map(roman.toUpperCase().split(''), (letter) => romanUnits[letter] ?? 0)
  return pipe(
    units,
    Array.map((unit, index) => (unit < (units[index + 1] ?? 0) ? -unit : unit)),
    Array.reduce(0, (sum, unit) => sum + unit),
  )
}

const identityOf = (part: PartDecl): string => (part.number === '' ? part.name : part.number)

const samePart = (part: PartDecl, other: PartDecl): boolean =>
  part.book === other.book && identityOf(part) === identityOf(other)

const dedupeParts = (decls: ReadonlyArray<PartDecl>): ReadonlyArray<PartDecl> =>
  Array.reduce(decls, [] as ReadonlyArray<PartDecl>, (kept, decl) =>
    Array.some(kept, (seen) => samePart(seen, decl)) ? kept : [...kept, decl],
  )

const byPart: Order.Order<PartDecl> = Order.combineAll([
  Order.mapInput(Order.Number, (part: PartDecl) => (part.number === '' ? 1 : 0)),
  Order.mapInput(Order.Number, (part: PartDecl) =>
    part.number === '' ? 0 : romanValue(part.number),
  ),
  Order.mapInput(Order.String, (part: PartDecl) => part.name),
])

type Numbered = { readonly number: string; readonly title: string }

const byNumber: Order.Order<Numbered> = Order.combineAll([
  Order.mapInput(Order.Number, (entry: Numbered) => (entry.number === '' ? 1 : 0)),
  Order.mapInput(Order.Number, (entry: Numbered) =>
    entry.number === '' ? 0 : Number(entry.number),
  ),
  Order.mapInput(Order.String, (entry: Numbered) => entry.title),
])

const chaptersUnder = (
  chapters: ReadonlyArray<Chapter>,
  part: PartDecl,
): ReadonlyArray<TocChapter> =>
  pipe(
    Array.filter(
      chapters,
      (chapter) => chapter.book === part.book && chapter.partId === identityOf(part),
    ),
    Array.sort(byNumber),
    Array.map(toTocChapter),
  )

## The discussions at the back

A discussion stands in its part beside the chapters, but the contents list it apart from them, at the back of its book under a `Back matter` heading. `discussionDeclOf` reads a discussion the way `chapterDeclOf` reads a chapter: its book, its part, its own number, its title, and its path. A discussion that names no part has no part to list it under, so its place stays empty, and it follows the discussions that name one.

`byDiscussion` orders the discussions by their parts first, as `byPart` orders the parts, and then by their own numbers, as `byNumber` orders chapters. `toTocDiscussion` writes the discussion's part as its place.

The orphan chapters stand last in the body, under a heading of their own, `Orphan chapters`, which `orphansHeading` holds beside `backMatterHeading` for the presentations that draw them.

=>

type Discussion = {
  readonly book: string
  readonly part: PartDecl
  readonly number: string
  readonly title: string
  readonly path: string
}

export const backMatterHeading = 'Back matter'

export const orphansHeading = 'Orphan chapters'

const discussionDeclOf = (module: LoomModule): Option.Option<Discussion> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter),
    Option.flatMap((frontmatter) =>
      Option.map(
        Option.all({
          discussion: Option.fromNullishOr(frontmatter.discussion),
          title: Option.fromNullishOr(frontmatter.title),
        }),
        ({ discussion, title }) => ({
          book: bookNameOf(module),
          part: {
            book: bookNameOf(module),
            number: frontmatter.part?.value ?? '',
            name: frontmatter.partName?.value ?? '',
          },
          number: discussion.value,
          title: title.value,
          path: module.path,
        }),
      ),
    ),
  )

const byDiscussion: Order.Order<Discussion> = Order.combineAll([
  Order.mapInput(byPart, (discussion: Discussion) => discussion.part),
  byNumber,
])

const placeOf = (part: PartDecl): string => (part.number === '' ? part.name : `Part ${part.number}`)

const toTocDiscussion = (discussion: Discussion): TocDiscussion =>
  TocDiscussionSchema.make({
    place: placeOf(discussion.part),
    number: discussion.number,
    title: discussion.title,
    path: discussion.path,
  })

## What the builder draws on

The builder reads the Loom AST and writes the contents model, so it draws from both. From `effect` come the array and option combinators, `Order` for the sort, `pipe` for the spine, and `Schema` for the model. From the Loom side come the corpus, the module a chapter is, and the `Location` of the root, with `bookOf`, which reads the book a loom names, and `isBookLoom`, which tells a loom that opens a book.

=>

import { Array, Option, Order, pipe, Schema } from 'effect'
import {
  bookOf,
  isBookLoom,
  type Location,
  type LoomCorpusAst,
  type LoomModule,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomCorpusAst'

